;;;-*- Mode:LISP; Package:FS; Base:10 -*-

;;; SYS: at the MIT site. OZ serves the sources at /sys and this site's own
;;; files at /site. Targets are host-less: the physical host is OZ.
;;;
;;; Every slash is doubled, and this file names no readtable, on purpose.
;;; A cold load reads it with MINI-READFILE (SYS: COLD; MINI), which does not
;;; look at a file's attribute list, so the file is read in the traditional
;;; readtable whatever the attribute line says. There a slash escapes the next
;;; character: with single slashes the strings below run together, a string is
;;; left unterminated, and the cold load stops with "End of file ... in the
;;; middle of the list". MIT's own site file never met this, its translation
;;; targets being TOPS-20 paths with no slashes in them.
;;;
;;; Spaces only, and no page marks: MINI's character stream hands the server's
;;; bytes to the reader untranslated (SYS: COLD; MINI, MINI-ASCII-STREAM),
;;; unlike the streams the file system uses later, which map ASCII 10, 11, 12,
;;; 14, 15 and 177 to the machine's own Backspace, Tab, Line, Page, Newline and
;;; Rubout (SYS: IO; STREAM, TYI-FROM-ASCII-STREAM). An ASCII tab therefore
;;; reaches the reader as a character the traditional readtable does not class
;;; as whitespace, and a line that begins with one is read as part of the token
;;; above it: the form quietly acquires an argument, and the error comes later
;;; and elsewhere. The sources QLD reads after the handover are full of tabs and
;;; page marks, and are fine, because they arrive through the translating
;;; streams. Only what the cold load reads over MINI is bound by this.

(SET-LOGICAL-PATHNAME-HOST "SYS"
  :PHYSICAL-HOST "OZ"
  :TRANSLATIONS '(("SITE;" "//site//")
                  ("*; *; *;" "//sys//*//*//*//")
                  ("*; *;" "//sys//*//*//")
                  ("*;" "//sys//*//")))
